Create a Python script that builds all service images, creates the KinD cluster, loads images, and applies all manifests in the correct order.

files:
- plat_scripts/deploy.py

deploy.py:
use subprocess and pathlib, no third-party dependencies.
resolve the project root as two levels up from this script's location.
create KinD cluster named svp if it does not already exist, check with kind get clusters.
build docker image svp/customer-user-service:latest from customer_user_service/Dockerfile, context customer_user_service/.
build docker image svp/planning-service:latest from planning_service/Dockerfile, context planning_service/.
build docker image svp/expense-service:latest from expense_service/Dockerfile, context expense_service/.
build docker image svp/frontend-service:latest from frontend_service/Dockerfile, context frontend_service/.
load all four images into the KinD cluster with kind load docker-image --name svp.
apply k8s/namespace.yaml.
apply k8s/configmap.yaml then k8s/secret.yaml.
apply all files in k8s/postgres/, k8s/redis/, k8s/kafka/.
wait for StatefulSets customer-user-db, planning-db, expense-db, redis, zookeeper, kafka to be ready in namespace svp using kubectl rollout status.
apply all files in k8s/customer-user-service/, k8s/planning-service/, k8s/expense-service/, k8s/frontend-service/.
print kubectl get pods -n svp at the end.
print a clear status message before each major step.
raise an exception and exit with code 1 on any subprocess failure.
use a run() helper that prints the command before executing it and streams output in real time.

imports / dependencies:
subprocess, pathlib, sys from standard library only.
KinD cluster name: svp.
all manifests under k8s/ relative to project root.
images tagged svp/<service-name>:latest.
kubectl and kind must be installed and on PATH.

standards:
follow kubernetes best practices.
use resource requests and limits on all containers.
add liveness and readiness probes where appropriate.